WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.662
Proving it starts with logs, because
Entra ID keeps them only briefly.

2
00:00:04.662 --> 00:00:10.625
Sign-in and audit logs are kept seven days
on the free tier and thirty on P1 or P2.

3
00:00:10.625 --> 00:00:14.275
Risky sign-ins reach
ninety days only with P2.

4
00:00:14.275 --> 00:00:18.913
Microsoft Graph activity logs aren't
retained at all; they're export only.

5
00:00:18.913 --> 00:00:21.900
And upgrading a license
doesn't recover older data.

6
00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:25.791
So stream every category to
a Log Analytics workspace and

7
00:00:25.791 --> 00:00:29.600
keep sign-in and audit logs
three hundred sixty-five days.

8
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:33.893
On top of that, scheduled detections:
stale accounts over ninety days;

9
00:00:33.893 --> 00:00:37.509
PIM activation between ten at
night and six in the morning,

10
00:00:37.509 --> 00:00:42.781
after converting from UTC; interactive
sign-ins with no Conditional Access applied;

11
00:00:42.781 --> 00:00:48.129
application credentials expiring within
thirty days; any emergency account sign-in,

12
00:00:48.129 --> 00:00:53.100
which is critical; and more than fifty
membership changes to one group in an hour.

13
00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:58.434
For compliance, Compliance Manager has
templates for NIST 800-53 Revision 5 and

14
00:00:58.434 --> 00:01:02.474
FedRAMP Moderate, but they don't
produce indicator evidence,

15
00:01:02.474 --> 00:01:04.737
so treat them as a readiness check.

16
00:01:04.737 --> 00:01:08.777
Under FedRAMP 20x's
Consolidated Rules for 2026,

17
00:01:08.777 --> 00:01:12.175
the controls map to Key
Security Indicators.

18
00:01:12.175 --> 00:01:15.752
Account management maps to
automating account management,

19
00:01:15.752 --> 00:01:18.113
evidenced by the workflows and reviews.

20
00:01:18.113 --> 00:01:21.607
Least privilege maps to
ensuring least privilege and

21
00:01:21.607 --> 00:01:25.024
authorizing just-in-time,
evidenced by groups and PIM.

22
00:01:25.024 --> 00:01:28.737
Authentication maps to
adopting passwordless methods.

23
00:01:28.737 --> 00:01:33.119
Non-user authentication maps to
securing non-user authentication,

24
00:01:33.119 --> 00:01:34.762
the service account work.

25
00:01:34.762 --> 00:01:39.063
And event logging maps to logging
event types and reviewing logs.

26
00:01:39.063 --> 00:01:40.475
Drift lives in Git.

27
00:01:40.475 --> 00:01:44.882
The identity assessment commits its
JSON to an identity baseline branch,

28
00:01:44.882 --> 00:01:48.711
a weekly scan on Sunday at two in
the morning compares against it,

29
00:01:48.711 --> 00:01:50.950
and drift opens an issue with the diff.

30
00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:55.487
The rule: close each finding at the
source, never by editing the copy.

31
00:01:55.487 --> 00:02:00.801
Be clear about maturity: the comparison
covers user additions and removals today;

32
00:02:00.801 --> 00:02:04.220
attribute, membership and
policy diffs are planned.
